abstract: >-
  Simulation code, derived data and figures for a study of the energy and carbon
  side of communication scheduling in distributed AI training. A 512-accelerator
  data-parallel training pod (compute phase plus all-reduce communication phase)
  is coupled to a weather-driven PUE facility model at a 2 266 m plateau site
  (Xining) and to two illustrative hourly grid carbon-intensity profiles. The
  synchronisation interval k is a genuine energy lever: at liquid fraction
  f = 0.80 the fastest schedule (k = 64) is 7.4 % faster than the most frequent
  (k = 1) but emits 14.1 % more carbon, while the mean PUE of both is identical
  to five decimal places (1.14901). The other levers are quantified on the same
  model: liquid fraction (3.5 %), grid scenario (5.9x, 123 vs 727 g/kWh) and
  start-hour scheduling (50 %). The makespan-carbon trade-off exists only if
  sparse synchronisation costs convergence; the crossover staleness penalty is
  derived analytically as beta* = B/(A + B/k) = 0.114 (numerically 0.116).
  The facility PUE model is shared with the companion preprint
  doi:10.5281/zenodo.22743562.
